======================================================================
Part 1: linearity of the Phi -> Psi map via (BB-Psi'), symbolic
======================================================================
I[a*Phi1+b*Phi2] - (a*I[Phi1]+b*I[Phi2]) = 0  (should be 0: integral is linear)
Psi[Phi] is defined as an integral (in u) of I[Phi](x+u,y) times a
Phi-INDEPENDENT kernel e^{-u^2/2-u(x+y)} -- linear in I[Phi], hence linear in Phi,
by the SAME trivial linearity-of-integration argument. So Psi[a*Phi1+b*Phi2] =
a*Psi[Phi1]+b*Psi[Phi2] EXACTLY, for the SAME reason I[.] is linear.

Special case a=1,b=0, 'Phi2:=0' (i.e. Delta Phi := Phi1-0=Phi1, Delta Psi:=Psi1-0=Psi1):
the ALREADY-PROVEN (DISC-DEC-100 Sec 8.2) bound sup|Delta Psi|<=||Delta Phi||_inf
applied to this special case gives sup|Psi1|<=sup|Phi1|, i.e. M_Psi<=M_Phi.
(Legitimate: Phi2=0 trivially satisfies (BB-Psi') with Psi2=I2=0, so the cited
 bound -- which the record states for arbitrary field PAIRS -- literally applies.)

