======================================================================
Part 3: the LOCAL pointwise form -- SELF-CAUGHT ERROR, then corrected
======================================================================
WRONG bound (sup over SAME x only) -- checking where it fails:
     x      y          |Psi|    WRONG bound   holds?
  0.00   0.50     0.29189237     0.12560695    False
  0.00   2.00     0.39720023     0.24157486    False
  0.50   1.00     0.41779500     0.35892279    False
  1.00   5.00     0.18681768     0.75883257     True
  2.00   0.30     0.05082988     0.08215240     True
  0.10  20.00     0.01761147     0.37493302     True
(3/6 violations above CONFIRM the naive same-x bound is genuinely false,
 not a numerical-precision artifact -- e.g. x=0,y=0.5: |Psi|=0.2919 vs
 wrong-bound=0.1256, a >2x violation, far beyond any quadrature error.)

CORRECTED bound: sup ranges over x'>=x (not just x'=x) AND y'<=y:
     x      y    |Psi| (exact)  corrected bound   holds?
  0.00   0.50     0.2918923678       0.42494089     True
  0.00   2.00     0.3972002292       0.81727191     True
  0.50   1.00     0.4177949961       0.50022831     True
  1.00   5.00     0.1868176840       0.78735401     True
  2.00   0.30     0.0508298800       0.11014255     True
  0.10  20.00     0.0176114716       0.96260327     True
CONFIRMED: the CORRECTED bound |Psi(x,y)|<=y*R(x+y)*sup_{x'>=x,y'<=y}|Phi(x',y')|
holds at every tested point. The x'-range must extend forward (to x'>=x,
unboundedly), not stay fixed at x -- Psi(x,y) is NOT causal in x. This
(anti-)causality structure is used again in ATTEMPT.md Sec 5.

ALL CHECKS PASSED (Parts 1-3). Summary:
 - Phi->Psi is LINEAR via (BB-Psi') (trivial, from integral linearity)
 - hence M_Psi<=M_Phi is an IMMEDIATE corollary (Phi2:=0 special case)
   of the ALREADY-PROVEN DISC-DEC-100 Sec 8.2 Lipschitz-<=1 bound --
   NOT claimed as new content, only made explicit for this front's use.
 - a sharper LOCAL (x,y-dependent, causal in y' via I) pointwise bound
   also holds, confirmed numerically to >=6 significant digits.
